# sentence_id = 102
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 8.3
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Homer, however, just as he differs in other respects, seems also to have understood this well, whether through art or through natural genius; for in composing the *Odyssey*, he did not poetize everything that happened to Odysseus—such as his being wounded on Parnassus or feigning madness at the gathering of the host, neither of which events made it necessary or probable that the other should occur—but constructed the *Odyssey* around a single action of the kind we describe; and similarly with the *Iliad*.
# literal_translation = Homer moreover, just-as also according-to other-things differs, and this seems well to-have-known either on-account-of art or on-account-of nature; Odyssey for making not poetized all whosoever to-himself happened, for-instance wounded indeed to-have-been in Parnassus, to-have-feigned moreover insane to-be in gathering, of-which no-one, another made, necessary or probable another to-become, but around one action of-what-sort we-say Odyssey constructed; similarly moreover and Iliad.
