# sentence_id = 103
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 8.4
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Just as in other imitative arts a single imitation is of a single subject, so too the plot, since it is an imitation of an action, must be an imitation of a single and whole action; and its component parts must be so arranged that if any part is transposed or removed, the whole is disrupted and disturbed; for that which makes no noticeable difference whether it is added or not added is no part of the whole.
# literal_translation = It-behooves therefore, just-as also in other imitative-arts one imitation of-one is, so also plot, since of-action imitation is, of-one-and to-be and of-this whole and parts to-consist of-things so, as transposed any part or removed to-differ and to-be-moved whole; which for added or non added nothing makes notable, no part of-whole is.
