# sentence_id = 190
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 15.11
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Since tragedy is an imitation of superior people, we ought to imitate good portrait painters; for they, while rendering the individual appearance and making subjects likenesses, paint them handsomer. So too the poet, when imitating men who are irascible or indolent or have other such traits in character, should portray them as noble while maintaining these traits; just as Agathon and Homer portrayed Achilles as a model of harshness.
# literal_translation = Since moreover imitation is tragedy of-better-ones, us behooves to-imitate good of-images painters; and for those assigning proper form similar making-ones better paint; so also poet imitating and irascible-ones and indolent-ones and other such-things having in characters such-ones being noble to-make; exemplar of-hardness as-if Achilles Agathon and Homer.
