# sentence_id = 201
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 16.9
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Fourth is that which arises from reasoning, as in the *Libation Bearers*: "Someone similar has come; no one is similar except Orestes; therefore he has come." And that of Polyeidus the sophist regarding Iphigenia: for it was natural for Orestes to reason that since his sister was sacrificed, it happened that he too was to be sacrificed. And in Theodectes' *Tydeus*, that coming as though to find his son, he himself perished. And the one in the *Phineidae*: seeing the place, they reasoned their fate, that it was fated for them to die in this place, for they had been exposed here.
# literal_translation = Fourth moreover which out-of syllogism, for-instance in Choephoroe, that similar someone came, similar moreover no-one but either Orestes; this-one therefore came. And which of-Polyeidus sophist concerning Iphigenia; probable for Orestes to-have-syllogized that if sister sacrificed is and to-himself to-happen to-be-sacrificed. And in Theodectes' Tydeus, that coming as-if to-find son himself perished. And which in Phineidae; seeing for place syllogized fate, that in this fated was to-die to-themselves, and for exposed were here.
