# sentence_id = 210
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 17.6
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = to lay them out universally, for example, that of *Iphigenia*: a certain maiden, when on the point of being sacrificed, disappeared mysteriously from her sacrificers, and was settled in another land where it was the custom to sacrifice strangers to the goddess, holding this priesthood; later, it happened that her brother arrived there (and the reason why the god gave an oracle telling him to go there and for what purpose lies outside the plot); upon arriving, he was captured and, when about to be sacrificed, recognized her—either as Euripides handled it or as Polyeidus made him say, reasonably enough, "So not only my sister, but I too was destined to be sacrificed!"—and from this came his salvation.
# literal_translation = to-expose universally, for-instance Iphigenia's; to-be-sacrificed a-certain girl and disappearing secretly from sacrificing-ones, demurring moreover into another region, in which law was strangers to-sacrifice to-goddess, this had sanctification; in-time moreover posterior to-brother happened to-come sanctification's (that because oracle had-given god on-account-of which cause outside universal to-come thither and to what moreover outside plot), coming moreover and captured to-be-sacrificed future recognized; whether as Euripides or as Polyeidus made, according-to probable saying that non therefore only sister but and himself behooved to-be-sacrificed; and hence salvation.
