# prose_translation = If someone takes the same iambic line composed by Aeschylus or Euripides and changes a single word—substituting a dialect word for a standard term—he will see that the one appears noble and the other low. For instance, Aeschylus in his *Philoctetes* wrote: "An ulcer eats the flesh of my foot"; whereas Euripides substituted "feasts upon" (thoinate) for "eats" (esthiei).
# literal_translation = And in dialect moreover and in metaphors and in other species transposing certainly someone proper nouns will-recognize that true-things we-say; for-instance same iambic poetizing Aeschylus and Euripides, one moreover sole name transposing, for proper customary dialect, this-one indeed seems beautiful this-one moreover vile is. Aeschylus indeed for in Philoctetes poetized «infirmity wolf which of-me flesh eats of-foot»; this-one moreover for 'to-eat' 'feasts-upon' transposed.
