# sentence_id = 308
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 23.5
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = On which account, as we have said already, Homer will appear admirable beyond all others in this respect as well, because he did not attempt to poetize the whole Trojan War, even though it had a beginning and an end; for the plot would have been excessively large and not easily comprehended as a whole, or if moderated in size, overly complex in its variety. As it is, selecting a single part, he has made use of many episodes from other parts—for instance, the Catalogue of Ships and other episodes with which he breaks the continuity of his poem.
# literal_translation = On-account-of which, as-if we-said already, and so admirable certainly will-appear Homer beyond others, by-that because neither war although having principle and end attempted to-poetize whole; exceedingly for certainly large and non easily conspicable was-owing to-fore magnitude moderating complex by-variety. Now moreover one part taking-away used superadventitious-things to-itself many, for-instance catalogue and other superinductitious-things twice twice breaks poetry.
