# sentence_id = 328
# document_id = 0086-034-lat
# subdoc = 24.18
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Homer especially taught other poets how to speak falsehoods properly—that is, through paralogism. For people think that when B exists or happens if A exists, then if B is present, A must also exist or happen; but this is false. Wherefore, even if the premise is false, it is not necessary for A to exist or happen or be added. For our soul, knowing that the second term is true, falsely infers that the first term is also true. An example of this is in the *Washing*.
# literal_translation = Taught moreover most Homer and others false-things to-say as-if . Is moreover this paralogism. Think for men, when this being or becoming this becomes, if which posterior is, and which prior to-be or to-become; this moreover is false. On-account-of which certainly, and if which first false, but neither this being necessary to-be or to-become or to-be-apposed. On-account-of this for to-know true to-which paralogized-is our soul and which first being. Example moreover to-this out-of Washing.
